# Independent Codex audit

Date: 2026-07-28

Status: remediation implemented 2026-07-28. The findings below describe the pre-fix tree and remain as the audit record.

Reviewed target: the current working tree at `0547aaf8461ecec636577d338cb649df7cdc5ec1`. Local `main` was one commit ahead of and two commits behind `origin/main` at review time. The pre-existing untracked `feedback.md` was not treated as evidence.

## Remediation summary

- Demo mode is explicit and development-only; production configuration fails closed. Protected routes use the Next proxy and server actor roles come from Supabase `app_metadata`.
- Event creation, event reads, profile ratings, and registration now use authenticated APIs. Postgres registration locks the occurrence, rejects duplicates/ineligible players, and requires a captured payment for paid entry.
- Stripe uses the official server SDK. The webhook verifies signatures and applies deduplicated database transitions; cron claims bounded batches, retries safely, and never reports invented captures.
- Vamos confirmations are user-bound, expiring, one-time, HMAC-checked, and database-backed in production. Supported match creation executes the shared repository command; unsupported actions return 501 without claiming success.
- Capacitor now uses an explicit hosted-HTTPS architecture with an offline fallback, no committed developer IP, no global arbitrary transport loads, and upgraded v8 native dependencies.
- Next/React/Supabase/Capacitor/Vitest were upgraded; CI now runs real Postgres/Redis migrations and concurrency checks plus typecheck, non-interactive lint, route tests, coverage thresholds, web build, and dependency audit.

External deployment still requires real Supabase, Postgres, Redis, Stripe, hosted HTTPS, Apple signing, and centralized telemetry credentials. Paid registration refuses entry until a verified payment exists; messaging remains clearly labeled device-only in demo and refuses to fake delivery in production. Checkout creation, relationship-scoped messaging/parent access, production Vamos data tools, and schedule queries remain intentionally disabled rather than simulated.

## Pre-fix executive verdict

This is a clickable mock/demo, not a production-capable application. Its most serious problem is not unfinished polish: the UI repeatedly reports that events were published, users were charged, registrations were committed, notifications were sent, and data was saved when no durable action occurred.

Do not enable real users, Stripe keys, or a release iOS build until the P0 items below are fixed. Existing tests provide false confidence because many assert copied logic, comments, strings, or file existence instead of exercising the shipped trust boundaries.

## P0 — release blockers

### 1. Core workflows report success without doing or persisting anything

Evidence:

- Event creation only displays a toast and closes the form (`src/app/(dashboard)/events/page.tsx:29-32`). It never adds the event even to local state.
- Paid registration says “Charged” but only sets a React boolean (`src/app/(dashboard)/events/[id]/page.tsx:40-47`). There is no payment, capacity check, registration row, or notification.
- Manual payment capture only changes component state (`src/app/(dashboard)/payments/page.tsx:38-40`).
- Profile save only displays a toast (`src/app/(dashboard)/profile/page.tsx:36`).
- Messages exist only in component state (`src/app/(dashboard)/messages/page.tsx:18-27`).
- “Add Partner” has no handler (`src/app/(dashboard)/community/page.tsx:28`).
- Schedule and dashboard data are hard-coded fixtures (`src/app/(dashboard)/schedule/page.tsx:7-12`, `src/app/(dashboard)/dashboard/page.tsx:12`).
- The repository contains no Supabase migrations/schema/RLS policies. The seed script explicitly only prints a message (`src/lib/seed.ts:1-27`).

Failure scenario: create an event, register and “pay,” save ratings, or send a message, then reload or navigate away. The claimed operation disappears. Other users never see it. For paid registration this is actively misleading: access is granted while no charge exists.

Fix:

1. Define a real schema and committed migrations for users/roles, events, occurrences, registrations, waitlist, conversations/messages, payments, and pending AI actions.
2. Add authenticated server-side commands for each write. Derive the actor, price, payee, and permissions from server-owned data.
3. Enforce authorization, validation, capacity, gender rules, and idempotency in the same transaction as the write.
4. Show success only after the authoritative command commits; refetch its result. Use explicit “demo simulation” language until this exists.

### 2. Authentication and authorization fail open

Evidence:

- Middleware logs and always calls `NextResponse.next()`; it never validates or refreshes a session (`src/middleware.ts:10-30`).
- Dashboard protection is a client-side truthiness check (`src/app/(dashboard)/layout.tsx:7-25`).
- In mock mode, `useAuth` accepts arbitrary JSON from `localStorage` as the current user (`src/hooks/useAuth.ts:24-30`). A visitor can set a coach or parent identity directly.
- Missing configuration, a URL containing `localhost`, or a URL containing `mock` silently selects the mock client (`src/lib/supabase/client.ts:126-136`). This can turn a production configuration error into no-auth mode.
- The mock-selection predicates disagree: `createClient()` checks for a missing key but `isMockMode()` does not (`src/lib/supabase/client.ts:126-142`). A real-looking URL with a missing key produces a login/redirect loop.
- Signup lets the client choose `coach`, `student`, or `parent` and stores it in mutable user metadata (`src/app/(auth)/signup/page.tsx:23-31`). Real-mode auth then trusts that metadata (`src/hooks/useAuth.ts:42-49`).
- `/api/vamos/chat` does not authenticate a user or scope returned player/payment data (`src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:25-62`). None of the API routes uses the supplied server Supabase client.

Failure scenario: a production env typo activates the local mock. An attacker sets `vamoverse_mock_user` to a fabricated coach and reaches protected pages. With real Supabase, users can self-assert privileged roles unless the database ignores this metadata. Direct API clients do not need a session at all.

Fix:

- Use one explicit `DEMO_MODE` switch that is rejected during production startup/build. Do not infer security mode from a URL substring.
- Validate required environment variables at startup and fail closed on incomplete configuration.
- Authenticate protected pages on the server with `auth.getUser()` and refresh cookies in middleware.
- Authenticate every API/command independently. Load roles, tenant/coach relationships, and parent/junior relationships from protected database records or server-managed `app_metadata`, never client-owned `user_metadata`.
- Add RLS policies and test them with multiple real users. Hiding navigation is not authorization.

### 3. Every live-money path is a facade

Evidence:

- Merely setting `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` selects `StripePaymentClient` (`src/lib/payments/client.ts:48-57`).
- That client makes no Stripe request but returns `captured`/`requires_capture`; capture and refund return unconditional success (`src/lib/payments/stripe.ts:7-33`).
- The webhook reads `stripe-signature` but never uses it. Both signature verification and event processing are comments, after which it returns HTTP 200 (`src/app/api/payments/webhook/route.ts:7-48`). Without a webhook secret it also accepts every POST, regardless of environment (`src/app/api/payments/webhook/route.ts:11-17`).
- The cron route authenticates in production, but all DB and Stripe work is commented pseudocode and the response always says `captured: 0` (`src/app/api/cron/capture/route.ts:41-71`).
- The visible payment screen does not use either payment client. Its auto-capture is a browser timer over a separate hard-coded array (`src/app/(dashboard)/payments/page.tsx:9-40`). Closing the tab loses the timer and state.
- `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is absent from `.env.example` (`.env.example:1-9`).

Failure scenario: an operator configures Stripe and believes the production adapter is active. The application reports capture/refund success without moving money. Stripe sees 200 webhook acknowledgements, so genuine payment failures, refunds, and disputes are silently discarded.

Fix:

- Until the real adapter exists, throw a startup/runtime error when live Stripe mode is selected; never fabricate financial success.
- Create PaymentIntents only in authenticated server code, using amounts and recipients from database records and durable idempotency keys.
- Persist a pending payment before the external call and reconcile it using a verified raw-body Stripe webhook. Store each Stripe event ID uniquely and apply only valid, idempotent state transitions.
- Implement bounded cron retries/claims without holding a database transaction open across a Stripe network call. Alert on old pending captures and job failures.
- Make webhook/DB state authoritative; the browser only renders it.

### 4. The iOS release path is not shippable

Evidence:

- Capacitor is permanently configured to load a developer LAN URL over cleartext HTTP (`capacitor.config.ts:7-10`). A release IPA will try `http://10.0.0.138:3000` instead of bundled assets.
- The tracked iOS plist enables `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` for every build despite its own “DEV ONLY” warning (`ios/App/App/Info.plist:48-58`).
- `webDir` is `out`, but `ios:build` and `ios:dev` run ordinary server-mode `next build`, which does not create/update `out` (`capacitor.config.ts:6`, `package.json:19-23`). `cap sync` can therefore package stale content.
- The only static command, `npm run ios:build:static`, currently fails: `/events/[id]` has no `generateStaticParams()` for `output: export` (`next.config.js:2-7`, `src/app/(dashboard)/events/[id]/page.tsx:9`).
- Even after that route is fixed, the static bundle calls relative Next API routes such as `/api/vamos/chat` (`src/app/(dashboard)/vamos/page.tsx:25`), which do not exist inside a static Capacitor bundle.

Independently reproduced result:

```text
npm run ios:build:static
Error: Page "/events/[id]" is missing "generateStaticParams()"
```

Fix: choose one supported mobile architecture and enforce it in CI:

- Bundled app: make all pages exportable and configure an external HTTPS API base URL; omit `server.url` from release builds.
- Hosted shell: intentionally point Capacitor at a deployed HTTPS application with an allowlist and documented review implications.

Keep live reload in a separate development config. A release build must start from a clean `out`, produce it successfully, package it, and smoke-test navigation/API calls offline from the developer LAN.

## P1 — major correctness and engineering risks

### 5. Vamos confirmation is broken for honest users and forgeable for direct callers

Evidence:

- Drafts return a raw `pending_action` but no server-issued ID (`src/lib/vamos/mock_engine.ts:132-152`, `src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:143-150`).
- The widget sends deprecated `CONFIRM:` text (`src/components/vamos/VamosWidget.tsx:60-63`), which the route explicitly rejects (`src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:133-140`).
- The full page sends a different free-text `CONFIRM ...` message instead of `confirmedActionId` (`src/app/(dashboard)/vamos/page.tsx:54-60`).
- A direct caller can forge `pa_<recent timestamp>_<any 16 chars>`. There is no HMAC, stored-action lookup, owner binding, payload binding, one-time consumption, or mutation (`src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:67-130`). Future timestamps are also accepted because only age is checked.
- The route then says “Done” even though it did not create a match or payment (`src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:123-130`).
- Setting `OPENAI_API_KEY` still runs the mock engine but labels the response `mock: false` (`src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:155-166`).
- Parsing/runtime failures return HTTP 200 and expose `err.message`, which defeats client retries and server error-rate monitoring (`src/app/api/vamos/chat/route.ts:168-171`).

Fix: generate a cryptographically random opaque ID; persist the exact validated action with user ID, expiry, and unconsumed status; return only that ID to the client. On confirmation, atomically consume it, reauthorize the user, revalidate current price/capacity/roster, execute the real command, and return the persisted result. Add auth, Zod request limits, rate limits, timeouts, and non-200 error responses before connecting a paid model/tool loop.

### 6. Business invariants are demonstrated but not enforced at the write boundary

Evidence:

- Registration never calls `registerWithCapacityCheck` or `validateDiscipline` (`src/app/(dashboard)/events/[id]/page.tsx:40-47`).
- The capacity helper takes a caller-supplied stale `currentCount` and has no shared state or lock (`src/lib/utils/capacity.ts:64-90`). Two callers can both register for the final slot.
- The displayed mixed-doubles roster contains two women and one man, but the UI says it needs one woman (`src/app/(dashboard)/events/[id]/page.tsx:34-38`, `:95-103`).
- Team balancing ignores discipline and gender and minimizes only combined UTR (`src/lib/utils/gender.ts:106-141`), so it may create men-vs-women teams for mixed doubles.
- Custom match creation validates a hard-coded fake roster and then claims a capacity transaction passed even though none ran (`src/app/(dashboard)/play/page.tsx:106-117`).
- Event form values accept `NaN`, negative price/capacity, reversed UTR ranges, and out-of-range ratings (`src/app/(dashboard)/events/page.tsx:57-66`). Installed Zod/react-hook-form packages are unused.

Fix: put duplicate prevention, capacity, waitlist order, eligibility, price, and roster composition into one server transaction/RPC. For mixed doubles, validate both the 2M/2F roster and one-M/one-F team composition. Treat client validation as usability only. Add schemas with numeric bounds and cross-field rules at both the form and server boundary.

### 7. Roles and promised parent safety features do not exist

Evidence:

- Every non-coach gets student navigation (`src/components/layout/AppShell.tsx:20-37`) and the student dashboard (`src/app/(dashboard)/dashboard/page.tsx:14`, `:81`).
- Signup promises parents junior management, payment visibility, and chat auditing (`src/app/(auth)/signup/page.tsx:91-95`), but no parent route or server data model implements them.
- “Create Event — Coach Only” is visible and executable without a role check (`src/app/(dashboard)/events/page.tsx:19-31`, `:46`). Students can directly visit `/payments` as well.
- The messages page says its permission matrix and junior audit are active, but it uses one static conversation list and has no permission checks (`src/app/(dashboard)/messages/page.tsx:8-33`, `:45-62`).

Fix: define server-owned role and relationship records, explicit parent experiences, and authorization policies for each query/mutation. Add multi-user tests proving students cannot perform coach actions, unrelated parents cannot inspect juniors, and authorized parents receive the required audit view.

### 8. The quality gates are designed to go green without validating the product

Independent command results:

```text
npm test:                     190 passed
npx tsc --noEmit:             failed with 5 errors
npm run build:                passed only after "Skipping validation of types" and "Skipping linting"
npm run lint:                 opened the interactive ESLint setup prompt (no committed config)
npm run test:coverage:        failed; @vitest/coverage-v8 is missing
npm run ios:build:static:     failed
```

Why the green tests are misleading:

- The “API integration” suite explicitly does not start Next or call the route (`tests/integration/api-routes.test.ts:3-6`). One webhook test is literally `expect(true).toBe(true)` (`tests/integration/api-routes.test.ts:9-17`).
- Cron auth is reimplemented inside the test instead of importing/calling the handler (`tests/integration/api-routes.test.ts:34-63`). The test and production code can drift together or independently.
- “Security invariants” read source text and pass if comments contain `HMAC`, `internal_review`, or similar words (`tests/smoke/build.test.ts:91-112`). This is why commented-out webhook/cron code is reported as covered.
- “Full App Smoke” tests utility functions, not pages, persistence, auth, browser behavior, or a running app (`tests/smoke/full-app.test.ts:6-69`).
- Production builds suppress type and lint failures (`next.config.js:13-18`). Current TypeScript errors include the event card type and untyped Supabase cookie adapter.
- There is no committed CI workflow.

Fix:

1. Remove `ignoreBuildErrors` and `ignoreDuringBuilds`; add non-interactive ESLint configuration.
2. Add CI gates for clean install, typecheck, lint, unit tests, coverage, web build, static/iOS build, and dependency audit.
3. Exercise real route handlers or a running application. Use a disposable Supabase database with migrations/RLS, Stripe test mode/webhook signatures, and browser tests with two or more identities.
4. Add adversarial tests: concurrent final-slot registration, duplicate payment retry, stale/replayed webhook, forged parent/coach access, expired/replayed pending action, missing/malformed env, reload persistence, and release mobile navigation.
5. Set coverage thresholds over application code; do not count source-string assertions as security tests.

### 9. Production dependencies have known high/critical advisories

A fresh `npm audit --omit=dev --audit-level=high` reported 10 vulnerabilities: 1 critical, 7 high, and 2 low. Important chains include the pinned Next.js 14.2.35 App Router/RSC server issues, old `@supabase/ssr`/`cookie`, PostCSS, and Capacitor CLI's `tar` chain. `@capacitor/cli` is incorrectly installed as a production dependency (`package.json:31-34`). `next.config.js:8-11` also permits every Server Action origin even though the application currently has no Server Actions.

Fix: plan supported-version upgrades rather than running a blind force update. Upgrade Next/React and Supabase SSR with their migration guides and regression tests; move build-only CLI packages to `devDependencies`; upgrade Capacitor/tar; regenerate the lockfile; remove wildcard Server Action origins. Block release on unresolved remotely reachable high/critical advisories, with documented exceptions only after reachability analysis.

## Highest-leverage improvement plan

### Phase 1: make behavior honest and modes explicit

- Rename the current experience “Demo simulation” everywhere.
- Add validated `demo`, `web-production`, and `mobile-development/release` configurations. Production must fail closed when auth, database, Stripe, API base URL, or signing secrets are incomplete.
- Disable or visibly mark every fake write; never say “charged,” “receipt sent,” “notified,” “saved,” or “created” for a local state change.

### Phase 2: deliver one real vertical slice

Implement event occurrence registration end to end before expanding breadth:

1. committed schema/migrations and RLS;
2. server-authenticated event read;
3. transactional capacity/eligibility/duplicate/waitlist registration;
4. real payment creation and webhook reconciliation for paid events;
5. durable UI result and reload persistence;
6. concurrent and cross-user tests.

This slice will establish the patterns needed by schedule, messaging, parent controls, and Vamos instead of creating more disconnected mocks.

### Phase 3: make automation a client of the same commands

Vamos must not have a separate privileged implementation. Its confirmed tools should call the same authenticated, validated, idempotent commands used by the UI. Store proposed actions, authorize at confirmation time, and audit the proposer, confirmer, exact payload, and result.

### Phase 4: choose and prove the mobile architecture

Resolve static bundle versus hosted shell, remove committed LAN/cleartext release settings, and add a clean-device release smoke test. Do not keep both paths half-configured.

### Phase 5: replace self-confirming tests with boundary tests

Keep focused unit tests for pure rating/gender helpers, but move confidence to real boundaries: session cookies, RLS, role separation, transactions under concurrency, Stripe signature/idempotency behavior, page reloads, and a packaged mobile build.

## Minimum production acceptance criteria

- No inferred/fallback mock mode in production.
- All protected reads and writes authenticate and authorize on the server; RLS is committed and tested.
- No UI success message precedes authoritative persistence/payment state.
- Stripe create/capture/refund/webhook flows work in test mode and fail closed when misconfigured.
- Concurrent registration cannot overbook or duplicate a user.
- Parent/junior privacy rules are enforced in storage and queries, not labels.
- Vamos confirmations are opaque, user-bound, payload-bound, expiring, one-time, and execute real commands.
- `typecheck`, non-interactive lint, tests, coverage, web build, and chosen iOS release build all pass from a clean checkout.
- No unresolved reachable critical/high production dependency advisory without a reviewed exception.
